g":0,"side":1}}],"legend":[["fill","cover","the Euclidean section from $\\sigma = \\pi/4$ to $3\\pi/4$, as far as a surface in flat space carries it"],["line","r","$\\sigma$ constant, at $\\pi/4$, $\\pi/3$, $2\\pi/3$ and $3\\pi/4$: the circle of Euclidean global time"],["line","throat","the neck $\\sigma = \\pi/2$, the smallest circle, of radius $L$, where the dilaton is least"],["line","meridian","$\\theta$ constant, every $15°$"]],"turn":{"origins":[[0.0,0.0]],"meridians":24,"tint":{"sheet":"cover"},"marks":[]}},"settings":"$L = 1$, the unit of every length, on the surface of $\\sigma$ and the angle $\\theta = it$ of period $2\\pi$, the period of the Euclidean global time.","stops":["Beyond $\\sigma = \\pi/4$ and $3\\pi/4$ the circles grow faster than the distance out to them, as $\\cosh$ of the proper distance from the neck, and no surface of revolution in flat space carries the slice."],"caption":["The Euclidean section of the wormhole with the global time made periodic, drawn as a surface in flat space with every distance along it the metric distance. With the time turned to the angle $\\theta = it$ of period $2\\pi$ the metric is $L^2(d\\sigma^2 + d\\theta^2)/\\sin^2\\sigma$, a cylinder whose circle at $\\sigma$ has the radius $L/\\sin\\sigma$, so the neck at $\\sigma = \\pi/2$ joins the two boundaries.","The surface is the low temperature phase of the coupled boundaries, thermal anti-de Sitter space, whose circle of Euclidean time never closes. Out from the neck the circles grow as $\\cosh$ of the proper distance, and at $\\sigma = \\pi/4$ and $3\\pi/4$, where the radius is $\\sqrt{2}\\,L$, the surface runs flat, the farthest a surface in flat space carries it.","The meridians $\\theta = 0$ and $\\theta = \\pi$ are the moment $t = 0$, the line along which the Euclidean section meets the wormhole."]}]}
